# Optimal Solution

Your solution is already the optimal `O(n + m)` approach. I would make only a small naming improvement:

```python
class Solution:
    def validSequence(self, word1: str, word2: str) -> list[int]:
        n = len(word1)
        m = len(word2)

        # last[j] = earliest index in word1
        # from which word2[j:] can be matched exactly
        last = [-1] * m

        i = n - 1
        j = m - 1

        while i >= 0 and j >= 0:
            if word1[i] == word2[j]:
                last[j] = i
                j -= 1
            i -= 1

        ans = []
        used_mismatch = False
        j = 0

        for i in range(n):
            if j == m:
                break

            # Case 1: exact match
            if word1[i] == word2[j]:
                ans.append(i)
                j += 1

            # Case 2: use the one allowed mismatch
            elif not used_mismatch:
                # After taking i, word2[j+1:] must
                # still be exactly matchable.
                if j == m - 1 or i < last[j + 1]:
                    ans.append(i)
                    used_mismatch = True
                    j += 1

        return ans if j == m else []
```

**TC:** `O(n + m)`
**SC:** `O(m)`

### Optimal Logic

```text
              Build last[]
                   |
                   v
        Scan word1 from left
                   |
                   v
        ┌──────────────────┐
        │ word1[i]==word2[j]? │
        └────────┬─────────┘
                 |
          +------+------+
          |             |
         YES            NO
          |             |
          v             v
       Take i      mismatch unused?
       j += 1           |
                    +----+----+
                    |         |
                   NO        YES
                    |         |
                   Skip       Check
                              i < last[j+1]
                              |
                         +----+----+
                         |         |
                        NO        YES
                         |         |
                        Skip      Take i
                                  mismatch used
```

### Why `i < last[j+1]`?

Suppose we want to use `i` as the mismatch.

After taking `i`, we still need:

```text
word2[j+1:]
```

to match **exactly**.

`last[j+1]` tells us the earliest position where the next required character can be taken while preserving the whole suffix.

So:

```python
i < last[j + 1]
```

means:

> There is still enough room after `i` to construct the remaining exact subsequence.

That condition is what makes the greedy choice safe.

